---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import buildInfo from '../data/meta.json';
import programs from '../data/programs.json';
import tax from '../data/taxonomy.json';
import { DIMS, type Program, type Taxonomy } from '../lib/data';
const T = tax as unknown as Taxonomy;
const P = (programs as Program[]).slice().sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Base title="About" description="How the Communication PhD Explorer collects programs and faculty, labels research, and matches your statement in the browser.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About this explorer</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:12px">A free tool for people applying to US doctoral programs in Communication, Media Studies and Journalism. It is modeled on the <a href="https://political-science-program-explorer.vw9pwn.chatgpt.site/" rel="noreferrer">Political Science Program Explorer</a> and rebuilt for Communication with program-level results, explainable matching, a shortlist and a bilingual interface. Built by <a href="https://junyuj.com" rel="noreferrer">Junyu Jiang</a>.</p>

    <h2>What is in the data</h2>
    <p>{buildInfo.programs} universities and {buildInfo.faculty} tenure-line faculty (assistant, associate and full professors) from the units that grant a research PhD in communication, media or journalism. Lecturers, teaching and clinical faculty, professors of practice, emeriti, visiting and affiliated faculty are not included, because the tool is about who can supervise a dissertation. Data was read from official department directories on {buildInfo.built}.</p>
    <h3>How the programs were chosen</h3>
    <p>Candidates came from the <a href="https://www.natcom.org/doctoral-program-guide/" rel="noreferrer">NCA Doctoral Program Guide</a> and from the US universities in the ShanghaiRanking 2025 Communication subject list, which was used only to find candidates. Each candidate was then checked against its official pages for a research doctorate; universities that only offer master’s degrees were dropped. The site shows no rankings: they say little about whether a department fits your research.</p>
    <h3>What is published about each person</h3>
    <p>A one-sentence research summary written for this site, the research areas the department lists, the research labels below, and links to the official department profile and, where the department links them, a personal site or Google Scholar. No photos are stored or shown, and profile text from department pages is not republished: it is read once, at build time, to compute the matching vectors, and only those vectors are published.</p>
    <h3>Research labels</h3>
    <p>Every faculty member carries labels in five dimensions, assigned by a language model from the public profile text (department profile, personal site when linked, listed research areas). A ★ marks a primary emphasis. Labels are interpretive aids: they describe documented emphases, not the whole of anyone’s work, and they can be wrong. Each record states its sources and a confidence level.</p>
    <div class="rows" style="margin:12px 0">
      {DIMS.map((d) => <div class="panel"><b>{T.dimensions[d].label}</b> <span class="muted">— {T.dimensions[d].description}</span><div class="chips" style="margin-top:8px">{Object.entries(T.dimensions[d].labels).map(([c, l]) => <span class="chip" data-dim={d}>{l}</span>)}</div></div>)}
    </div>

    <h2>How matching works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Semantic score.</b> Each faculty member is represented by one 768-dimensional <code>snowflake-arctic-embed-m-v1.5</code> vector, the average of three views: the one-sentence research summary with keywords, the department profile text (used only to compute this vector, never published), and the published summary, research areas and labels. Faculty vectors are precomputed and stored as 8-bit integers; your text is embedded in your browser with <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js" rel="noreferrer">Transformers.js</a> (a one-time download of about 110 MB, cached by the browser).</li>
      <li><b>Keyword score.</b> BM25 over the published text of each profile.</li>
      <li><b>Fusion.</b> Both scores are turned into z-scores within your query and combined 85% semantic, 15% keyword (adjustable). Regions, methods and topics named in your text become chips; a chip adds a small bonus to faculty who carry that label, a second click makes it a requirement, a third switches it off.</li>
      <li><b>Re-reading.</b> A small cross-encoder (<code>ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6</code>, 23 MB) reads your text against each of the top 30 profiles and the two orders are blended evenly. Results it moved up are marked.</li>
      <li><b>Chinese.</b> Text in Chinese is translated to English on your device, with your browser's built-in translator when it has one and otherwise a 110 MB translation model. Platform names and method terms (抖音, 微信群, 数字民族志 …) are added from a glossary as English keywords, and the translation is shown with the results so you can correct it.</li>
      <li><b>Fit</b> (0–100) is calibrated on 32 realistic probe descriptions: the median person for a typical query scores 0, the top 0.5% score 85, and the curve flattens above that. <b>Program fit</b> = 50% of the best match + 30% of the second + 20% of the third, so departments with several plausible advisors rank above departments with one star.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><b>How this was chosen.</b> The matcher was tuned on 40 test descriptions (30 statement-style, 6 short keyword, 4 Chinese) with 1,300 query–faculty pairs graded 0–3 by independent reviewers who saw only the published profiles. Against those grades (nDCG@10), the previous version scored 0.69 and this one 0.85, with Chinese queries going from 0.01 to about 0.7 (only four Chinese test queries, so treat that figure as rough). Larger models (up to 1.2 GB) did not do better. The test set and scripts are in <a href="https://github.com/Junyu-Jiang0508/communication_phd/tree/main/pipeline/eval" rel="noreferrer">pipeline/eval</a>.</p>
    <p>Nothing you type leaves your device. There are no accounts and no analytics on this site.</p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Matching is done in English. Chinese text is machine-translated first; check the translation shown with your results, and edit it if a term came out wrong.</li>
      <li>A short profile gives a short embedding: faculty with sparse public pages are under-matched, not absent.</li>
      <li>Directories change. Hires, departures and title changes after {buildInfo.built} are not reflected until the next build.</li>
      <li>Multi-unit colleges (for example Texas, Penn State, Michigan State, Colorado) list all PhD-granting units; check each unit’s own admission page for which faculty supervise which program.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>Every faculty page has a “Report an error” link that opens a GitHub issue; corrections are folded into the next build. Faculty who would like their information changed or removed can <a href="https://github.com/Junyu-Jiang0508/communication_phd/issues/new?template=data-correction.yml" rel="noreferrer">open an issue</a> or email <a href="mailto:junyujiang0508@gmail.com">junyujiang0508@gmail.com</a>, and the record will be updated or taken down. The code and the data pipeline are public at <a href="https://github.com/Junyu-Jiang0508/communication_phd" rel="noreferrer">github.com/Junyu-Jiang0508/communication_phd</a>.</p>

    <h2>Universities included</h2>
    <ul style="columns: 2; column-gap: 24px">{P.map((p) => <li><a href={`${base}programs/${p.slug}/`}>{p.name}</a> <span class="muted small">({p.n})</span></li>)}</ul>
  </article>
</Base>
